    {
      "id": "283",
      "type": "writeup",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 283,
        "body_ja": "",
        "body": "<p>化（か） by itself is a <span data-term='接尾語・せつびご'>suffix</span> in Japanese which means 'to change', or 'to transform'. When paired with <span data-gp-id='22'>する</span>, it means 'to transform into (A)', where (A) is the noun that 化（か） is connected to. This structure is regularly translated as '~ification', or '~ization', and similar <span data-term='接尾語・せつびご'>suffixes</span> in English.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='180824'>\r\n<li data-study-question='180825'>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>When the <span data-gp-id='22'>する</span> is connected to another noun, it may occasionally appear as した, or with <span data-gp-id='6'>の</span> attached to the end of it. This will indicate that there is a relationship between (B) and (A).</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'>\r\n<li data-study-question='180826'>\r\n<li data-study-question='180827'>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>However, in formal/scientific writing, the <span data-gp-id='22'>する</span> (and <span data-gp-id='6'>の</span>) may sometimes be dropped.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul class='writeup-examples--holder'><li data-study-question='180828'></ul>"
      }
    },
